my research clearly comes from my own  personal experiences and what who I am  and where I grew up and I think this is  the case for a lot of social scientists  in my case I was born in Europe to Dutch  parents I actually never lived in the  Netherlands as a young child we moved  around to a number of countries we live  briefly in Greece and Spain and in  England and then we migrated to Canada  when I was quite young I didn't speak a  word of English but quickly learned as a  young child went through the public  school system in Canada through to  university and then moved down to the  United States and have been a professor  in the University of California Berkeley  for the last eight years also during  that time I lived for a year in France  and I lived for a year in the  Netherlands and so all of those  migration experiences have definitely  made me someone who is very attentive to  issues of migration and identity and I  sometimes don't know how to answer this  question who are you and so last year  when I was in the Netherlands I was on  sabbatical and affiliated with the  Center for ethnic and migration studies  at the University of Amsterdam people  would ask me well where are you from and  it would be a very hard answer and so I  finally came down to I am North American  of Dutch background but it's a hard  thing to do and I think it's going to  get increasingly hard as people just  become even more mobile at the same time  in the u.s. context I continue to say  that I'm Canadian even though it's been  16 years that I haven't lived there
 

